   "use": "Often heard as care and advice, after drinking alcohol or in the heat: mizu nonda? (have you had some water?), mizu nonde ne (make sure you drink water). Also when thirsty: nodo kawaita, mizu nomitai (I'm thirsty, I want some water). Casual speech drops o.",
   "notes": "mizu is cold or room-temperature water; hot water is oyu, not atsui mizu. In restaurants ask for omizu, which is free. In polite heat warnings you hear komame ni suibun o totte kudasai (keep taking fluids).",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "atsui kara, mizu chanto nonde ne.",
    "ja": "暑いから、水ちゃんと飲んでね。",
    "english": "It's hot, so make sure you drink enough water."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "atsui node, omizu o chanto nonde kudasai ne.",
    "ja": "暑いので、お水をちゃんと飲んでくださいね。",
    "english": "It's hot, so please make sure you drink enough water."
   }
  },
